Z||000000||1FST2M6||01-25-2000||02:36 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>  <BR>The photo attached is a scan of my car, In 4th gear (Muncie 4spd) the Red line shows Nitrous on, the Blue Nitrous off. I also have one showing the differenc between running with the NOS progressive controller and with it off... it makes more torque with it off but has a broader band with it on... thats shown here. given the (Black GM Performance book at local Pontiac dealer) 27.5% drive line loss that would put these numbers at the flywheel.<BR>NO nos         155.2hp  202.4lb-ft<BR>Nitrous        208.8hp  300.7lb-ft<BR>not shown is the no controller with was 147.6hp and 243.7lb-ft at the wheels. Not trying to brag just letting you know what your 2.8 can really belt out when tuned. please note my car as of this morning on the way to work turned 96000 miles. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||dogtired||01-25-2000||03:18 PM||||COOL Chart, I don't have a clue what it says about a Fiero! Guess that is why I just use my seat of the pants Meter <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But from a Business point of view, The lower red line is the one to go with! Lets see buy in at 40 and sell out at 110.<BR>You certainly wouldn't want to go with the blue line, buying in at 225 and then at 6.0 drop off the chart??? Lost your shirt on that one??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But you know that is the way the market is!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I am re-reading your post?<BR>"The photo attached is a scan of my car." ??<BR>Ohhhh   OK  I get it!<BR>The top blue line is the top of your car where the lower red line is the ground effects. Car is going from left to right? The left side is actually the GT tail with a wing and the right is a pointy nose and at 6.0 you hit a brick wall?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>OK OK OK Just having fun!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Those are some impressive numbers! I really don't know anything about NOS? The kits to install it do not seem all that expensieve? What does it cost to refill a bottle?? And is it true that it shortens the life of a motor, due to the extra boost or is that just a load??? Again above, Was just having FUN!||||reg||6
Z||000002||1FST2M6||01-25-2000||04:45 PM||||Well, thanks for the jest!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Not really sure about the life of the motor issue that everyone speaks of. I haven't killed mine.. YET... I filled my 14th bottle yesterday afternoon. prices went up this year from $18 a bottle to an astronomical $31 a bottle! Its a 10 lb bottle. Its time to invest in the magnusen M-92 Supercharger. I'll have paid for it by the end of the year at this pace. From $36 a month to $62 a month... OUCH! thats dinner!... TWICE!  And for those that don't have a clue what the graph is. The 235.9lb-ft is more than a 95 Cobra lays down to the ground at any RPM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Torque makes you quick, HP makes you fast... I pick on them at a light not on the highway! Oh and for those of you new to the speed scene.. know how to pick your battles... Don't pick a fight you know you can't win. It looks better to to the home crowd when you have a winning record... OR buy a nitrous kit and play with EVERYONE! Kit retails for about $500 takes about 5 hours to put in and you should expect to replace tires often!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||01-25-2000||06:56 PM||||2m6,<P>What am I missing?  Why do you not register T or bhp from around 2k - 2.8k on the laughing gas curve?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000004||baggy||01-25-2000||09:10 PM||||I think I like racin' sombody to the river..<BR>or out on one of the mine roads..<BR>my stock little red racer will out corner almost anything...<BR>and..<BR>I have just enough power to pull the curves good without swappin' ends and goin' back in the other direction..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||01-25-2000||10:57 PM||||I think that anyone who has invested the time and money that you obviously have in their ride has earned the right to brag a bit! :-)<P>I am also a nitrous fan, it is a very effective means for gaining significant power, as long as it is done "right".  But I would warn those who desire to slap a $500 100hp shot kit on and "go for it", that problems could result.  There are important areas that need to be addressed to ensure the longevity of a motor under these conditions.  <P>I am particularly impressed in this case, as it was employed on an engine with a stock bottom end.  The use of a progressive NO2 controller, an enhanced fuel delivery system, and an ignition subsystem to retard the timing during NO2 injection, are  important aspects of a successful application on such a powerplant.  A well designed NO2 system such as this, would stress the components for a much shorter duration than the near full time boost situation that a turbo or supercharger develops.  It seems to me that the results were well worth the effort!<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||01-25-2000||11:30 PM||||Oh,and I thought it was the start of a J.J.Walker fan club.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||01-26-2000||08:13 AM||||I'm missing this area cause I had burned through a spark plug wire. And the computer on the Dyno dead shorted for that range of time. I have since replaced this wire. The dyno reads spark and rpm of the 4800lb drum to measure torque and HP if it can't read this spark it can't calculate these numbers. It didn't effect the HP or torque numbers with this bad wire. It was burned not totaly shorting out on the block but enough to make the computer flake.      more info on the dyno and how it works at <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||01-26-2000||09:24 AM||||Brock Yates wrote a really good article on Dynojet in Car and Driver about a year ago.  He had nothing but praise for Dynojet and how accurate it is.  Yates also related some funny stories about guys who would claim outrageous HP figures like 400-500 hp, then would be humbled to see they were really producing around 250 hp.<P>I think you can feel pretty darn good about your l'il 2.8!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||01-26-2000||09:58 AM||||Saw many humbled people around the dyno booth on Power Tour.||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||01-26-2000||10:36 AM||||Thanks a lot!! Yeah its pretty uplifting to see some kid in the shop on the weekends with their new car and dads credit card get shot down in flames when they realize that thier not NEARLY as big and bad as they thought...  Everyone needs a Fiero... The ultimate sleeper...   wanna go play?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
